s a modest lead, markets price him near 80%, and the midterm environment favors Democrats, outweighing Mackenzie's fundraising and incumbency advantages in a district that barely voted for him two years ago.", "watch": "A shift in the national environment or Mackenzie consolidating Republican-leaning blue-collar and Latino voters could tighten this considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ryan Mackenzie (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Ryan Mackenzie defends a Lehigh Valley seat he won narrowly two years ago, facing Democrat Bob Brooks. The district leans slightly Republican on paper, but the midterm environment and Trump's low approval in Pennsylvania are working against the incumbent. A September Franklin & Marshall poll shows Brooks ahead 45-40, and earlier Democratic-sponsored polling found a similar margin.\nMackenzie holds a clear money advantage, with more cash on hand and no significant outside spending against him, while Brooks has drawn substantial outside support. Expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean/Likely D, and prediction markets price Brooks near 80%. The race sits as a genuine but Democratic-tilted contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7234, "margin": 3.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7148, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7673, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7925, "margin": 4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7174, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7271, "margin": 4.2}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7743, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7833, "margin": 4.2}]}